In this lesson we'll look at translations, which is just a fancy word for sliding a shape to a new spot on the grid. The big idea is super simple: every point of the shape moves by the exact same horizontal amount and the exact same vertical amount, and that's it. Nothing tilts, nothing stretches, nothing turns. So how do you actually do one? You take a point and shift it right or left, then up or down. If you tell it to go, say, three to the right and two up, then every other vertex gets the same instructions. Positive means right or up, negative means left or down. Once you've shifted all the corners, just connect them again and you've got your new shape. The rule for any point is that its new x is the old x plus the horizontal shift, and the new y is the old y plus the vertical shift. The shape you end up with is congruent to the original, so it has the same size and the same orientation, just in a different place. Now let's see it in action.